    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Same grid as the row-major lesson, different memory. Reading the line left to right now gives 1, 4, 2, 5, 3, 6 — down column 0 first, then column 1, then column 2."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The formula"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "offset of grid[r][c]  =  c x ROWS + r"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It is the mirror image. `c x ROWS` skips over `c` complete columns, then `r` walks down that column. Note that it multiplies by the number of **rows**, not columns — a detail that is easy to get backwards."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For `grid[1][2]` in a 2 x 3 grid: `2 x 2 + 1 = 5`. Slot 5 holds 6 — the same value as before, found at the same slot by coincidence of this small example, but by an entirely different route."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Who uses it"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Column-major belongs mainly to the numerical and scientific world: Fortran, MATLAB, R, and Julia, plus linear algebra libraries like BLAS and LAPACK that were originally written in Fortran. NumPy can be asked for it with `order='F'` — F for Fortran."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The reason is historical but also practical: many linear algebra operations are naturally expressed in terms of columns, so a column-major layout makes those the fast path."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why you should care"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two reasons, even if you never write Fortran."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Crossing library boundaries.** Hand a row-major buffer to a column-major library and you get silently transposed results — no crash, just wrong numbers.",
        "**It sharpens the main idea.** The point is not which layout is better. It is that *some* layout always exists, and your loop order should match it."
      ]
    }
  ],
